    a: 'CDSCO (the Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation, headed by the DCGI) sits at the centre of drug, biologic, and device approval in India, working under the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules 2019 and the Medical Device Rules 2017. Biosimilar approval also involves the Review Committee on Genetic Manipulation (RCGM) under the Department of Biotechnology. Verify current requirements on the official CDSCO portal before treating any rule as fixed, since regulations get revised.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the real starting salary for medical biotechnology roles?',
    a: 'It varies sharply by role: roughly Rs 1.8-4 LPA for entry clinical-trial support roles, Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA for QC and pharmacovigilance roles, Rs 2.6-4.4 LPA for entry regulatory affairs, and Rs 3.4-6.3 LPA for bioinformatics/genomics analyst roles. See biotechnology careers salary and clinical research career path India for the full role-by-role and experience-level breakdown.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is molecular diagnostics the same as clinical research?',
    a: 'No. Molecular diagnostics and genetic testing involve running and interpreting lab tests (PCR, NGS) that support a diagnosis. Clinical research involves running the trials that test whether a new drug or treatment works and is safe. Both sit inside medical biotechnology, but they are different daily jobs with different employers and skill demands.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from a career in pharmacy?',
    a: 'Pharmacy (D.Pharm, B.Pharm, Pharm.D) is a licensed profession registered with the Pharmacy Council of India, built around dispensing, clinical pharmacology, and drug counselling. Biotechnology is a science degree with no licensing requirement, built around lab technique, molecular biology, and research or industry application. The two overlap heavily inside regulatory affairs, QA/QC, and pharmacovigilance, where either degree can lead to a similar role - see career in pharmacy India for the pharmacy-specific path.',
  },
];

